# Tick-mode lockstep regression
#
# Variant of emulator_smoke that opts into deterministic-time lockstep:
# the bridge advances simavr only when klippy's reactor sends an
# `advance T` over its tick socket, and klippy doesn't process timers
# or fds past T until simavr has advanced there. This eliminates the
# host-load timing skew that makes the parallel sim_time path flaky on
# CPU-contended hosts. We reuse emulator_smoke.cfg unchanged - the
# only difference is the fixture's `tick_mode: true` flag.
#
# KNOWN BLOCKED under --force-emulator (clean-host finding): tick-mode
# lockstep livelocks during identify. The bridge's UART byte shuttle is
# not synchronized with each RunFor - klippy writes identify bytes via
# the serialqueue C bg-thread (real-time poll) while the reactor advances
# sim time in jumps, so the AVR is never run *forward* over the bytes it
# was just sent. klippy then re-requests the identical advance target
# forever ("advance 0.395..." repeated) and the pty EOFs. Fixing this
# needs a synchronous shuttle (drain klippy TX -> RunFor -> drain MCU TX)
# in the simavr bridge plus a sim-time-aware serialqueue; until then this
# test fails when actually run (it was only ever skipped, since it is
# REQUIRES_EMULATOR and the standard CI run has no backend).
CONFIG emulator_smoke.cfg
DICTIONARY atmega2560.dict
EMULATOR emulator_tick_mode.fixture.json
REQUIRES_EMULATOR  # assertions depend on real MCU responses;
# skipped (not degraded to fileoutput) when the emulator backend isn't built

# A few harmless gcode lines so klippy gets past klippy:ready.
M114
M105
M105
M105

# klippy connected and stayed connected long enough to do periodic
# sht3x reads against the scripted fixture.
EXPECT_LOG_CONTAINS "Loaded MCU 'mcu'"
EXPECT_LOG_NOT_CONTAINS "Internal error"
EXPECT_LOG_NOT_CONTAINS "Klippy is shutdown"
